= Cognitive bias =

== Overview ==
A '''cognitive bias''' is a systematic tendency in judgment, memory, perception, or decision making that produces patterned deviation relative to a specified normative, statistical, or task standard.

Biases are not simply random mistakes. They are repeatable tendencies linked to how information is represented, searched, remembered, weighted, or compared.

== A benchmark is required ==
Calling a response biased requires a comparison standard.

Possible standards include:

* formal logic;
* probability theory;
* statistical estimation;
* consistency;
* task-specific accuracy;
* externally measured outcomes.

Different standards can produce different evaluations.

== Bias and heuristic ==
A '''heuristic''' is a simplified strategy.

A heuristic can produce a bias under one environment and be efficient or adaptive under another.

Therefore:

<pre>
cognitive bias ≠ heuristic
</pre>

The heuristics-and-biases tradition emphasizes systematic departures from normative benchmarks; ecological and fast-and-frugal approaches emphasize environments in which simple strategies work well.

== Examples ==
Examples in this wave include:

* [[Confirmation bias]];
* [[Anchoring effect]];
* [[Availability heuristic]];
* [[Framing effect]].

Other well-known phenomena include base-rate neglect, overconfidence, representativeness effects, and bias blind spot.

== Bias is not dishonesty ==
A cognitive bias can occur without conscious intention.

Deliberate deception, motivated advocacy, ignorance, and cognitive bias are different explanatory categories even when they produce similar conclusions.

== Cognitive and statistical bias ==
[[Selection bias]] is a methodological/statistical distortion caused by how observations enter an analysis.

It is not a cognitive bias merely because both use the word bias.

== References ==
* Amos Tversky & Daniel Kahneman, “Judgment under Uncertainty: Heuristics and Biases” (1974).
* Gerd Gigerenzer & Wolfgang Gaissmaier, “Heuristic Decision Making”, ''Annual Review of Psychology'' (2011).
* judgment and decision-making review literature.

